<h2 class="bde-heading-390-178 bde-heading">
What Does the Golden State Warriors’ Payroll Look Like in 2026-27?
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-211 bde-shortcode"><p>Golden State Warriors salaries across 15 standard contracts add up to <strong>$220.84 million</strong> in projected cap payroll for 2026–27. Curry leads at <strong>$62.59 million</strong>, followed by Butler at <strong>$56.83 million</strong> and Green at <strong>$27.68 million</strong>. Together they take roughly <strong>66.6%</strong> of that total.</p>
<p>That leaves around a third of the payroll for the other twelve standard contracts. Porzingis is the next-largest commitment at <strong>$20 million</strong>, while Moody’s salary reaches <strong>$12.5 million</strong>. The Warriors are concentrating most of their spending on the veterans around Curry, with smaller deals filling out the rotation.</p>
</div><div class="bde-shortcode-390-179 bde-shortcode"><div class="nst nst-component-cap" style="--nst-accent:#1D428A"><p>The Golden State Warriors standard roster accounts for $220.84M. Add $0.00M in dead cap to reach $220.84M before cap holds. Holds are separate accounting charges, not signed salary payments.</p><div class="nst-threshold-grid"><div><h3>Salary Cap</h3><strong>$164.96M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:100%"></i></div><p>$55.88M above<br><small>Using cap payroll before holds</small></p></div><div><h3>Luxury-Tax Line</h3><strong>$200.43M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:100%"></i></div><p>$20.41M above<br><small>Using tax salary</small></p></div><div><h3>First Apron</h3><strong>$209.02M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:100%"></i></div><p>$12.32M above<br><small>Using apron salary</small></p></div><div><h3>Second Apron</h3><strong>$221.69M</strong><div class="nst-threshold-track"><i style="width:99.843216080402%"></i></div><p>$0.35M below<br><small>Using apron salary</small></p></div></div><div class="nst-tax-panel"><h3>Estimated Luxury-Tax Bill</h3><div class="nst-tax-summary"><strong>$86.23M</strong><span>Repeater rates</span></div><p>Based on $220,838,432 in tax salary, $20,410,432 above the tax line. This is an estimate, not the final season-end assessment.</p><div class="nst-tax-history"><span><b>2022–23</b>Taxpayer</span><span><b>2023–24</b>Taxpayer</span><span><b>2024–25</b>Taxpayer</span><span><b>2025–26</b>Taxpayer</span></div><p class="nst-note">Three or more taxpayer seasons in the previous four trigger repeater rates. Status reviewed 2026-09-16.</p><details class="nst-accordion"><summary>How the Tax Estimate Is Calculated</summary><div class="nst-tax-bands"><div><span>$0.00M–$6.06M above tax</span><span>$3.00 per $1</span><b>$18,192,000</b></div><div><span>$6.06M–$12.13M above tax</span><span>$3.25 per $1</span><b>$19,708,000</b></div><div><span>$12.13M–$18.19M above tax</span><span>$5.50 per $1</span><b>$33,352,000</b></div><div><span>$18.19M–$20.41M above tax</span><span>$6.75 per $1</span><b>$14,974,416</b></div></div></details></div><h3>What Does Going Over the Tax Line Mean?</h3><p>The luxury tax is an additional team expense. It rises in bands as tax salary moves above the threshold; teams with a recent history of paying tax can face higher repeater rates. The final bill depends on the season-end calculation.</p><h3>Why the Aprons Matter</h3><p>Aprons affect how a team can build its roster, including certain exceptions and trades. Being below an apron does not automatically create cap space, and a payroll figure alone does not establish whether a hard cap has been triggered.</p><p class="nst-note">Cap holds: $33.50M. Threshold comparisons describe the recorded salary totals; they do not establish available signing room.</p></div></div>
</section><section id="team-eras" class="bde-fancy-container-390-172 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<h2 class="bde-heading-390-182 bde-heading">
Which Eras Cost the Warriors the Most?
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-183 bde-shortcode"><div class="nst nst-component-eras" style="--nst-accent:#1D428A"><details class="nst-accordion"><summary>1946 · Philadelphia Origins</summary><p>The Warriors began in Philadelphia in 1946.</p></details><details class="nst-accordion"><summary>2015–22 · Four Titles in Eight Seasons</summary><p>Golden State won championships in 2015, 2017, 2018 and 2022.</p></details><h3>The Payroll Over Time</h3><p>Across the four completed seasons shown, cap payroll ranged from $176.78M to $206.81M. The current roster puts 66.61% of active cap payroll into its three largest contracts.</p></div></div>
</section><section id="team-contracts" class="bde-fancy-container-390-173 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<h2 class="bde-heading-390-184 bde-heading">
Which Warriors Contracts Expire Next?
</h2><div class="bde-shortcode-390-212 bde-shortcode"><p>Butler and Green are both under contract through 2026–27, so Golden State faces big decisions next summer. Curry signed a two-year extension worth about <strong>$116 million</strong> in September that runs through 2028–29, with a player option in the final year, and Brandin Podziemski’s five-year, <strong>$110.00 million</strong> extension, agreed in October, runs to 2031–32. Porzingis, Horford and Melton hold player options for 2027–28.</p>
<p>Scheduled cap payroll for 2027–28 stands at <strong>$135.55 million</strong>, including options and non-guaranteed amounts, with Curry’s <strong>$55.70 million</strong> the largest piece. That gives the next round of signings a major role in shaping the Warriors’ future spending.</p>
